# 072.AmberFullModel - the complete AMBER model in BioSpring, one term at a time

Everything BioSpring can reproduce of a real AMBER ff99SB force field, on a
structure containing **all 20 standard amino acids**, split into four
cumulative stages you can run one after the other and feel the difference:

| stage | `make` target      | adds                                              |
|-------|--------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| 1     | `run-bonded`       | bonded only: stretching, bending, every dihedral  |
| 2     | `run-lj`           | + steric exclusion (Lennard-Jones 12-6)           |
| 3     | `run-lj-coulomb`   | + Coulomb electrostatics on real partial charges  |
| 4     | `run-full`         | + dynamic hydrogen bonds (`make run` default)     |

Build once with `make prep`, then switch stages freely: the bonded families
are a *build-time* choice baked into `model.nc` (a family not requested
never gets a spring created at all), while steric/Coulomb/hbond are
*runtime* choices living in the four `.msp` files. No rebuild between
stages.


## The structure

`gkinase.pdb`, copied from `example/001_GKinase` to keep this example
self-contained: 183 residues, 2814 atoms, and the only structure in the
repository that contains **every one of the 20 standard amino acids**. That
matters here — it is what makes this example exercise every rule in
`ProteinAtomBonded.bi.ff` at once, including the families that no smaller
test structure reaches (Trp's fused indole rings, Cys, Met's thioether).


## What stage 1 actually contains

Not just phi/psi/chi. The bonded model covers every proper torsion AMBER
defines for these residues, each as a ring of ghost-ghost springs calibrated
in closed form (see `doc/BondedForceFieldSprings.md`):

* backbone phi, psi, and omega;
* side-chain chi1-chi4, plus Arg's chi5 (NE-CZ);
* terminal rotors: methyls (Ala/Val/Ile/Leu/Thr/Met), hydroxyls (Ser/Thr/
  Tyr), Lys's ammonium;
* Asn/Gln side-chain amides and Arg's guanidinium C-N bonds — the same
  partial-double-bond torsion as the backbone peptide bond itself;
* the Proline pyrrolidine ring (which genuinely puckers, endo/exo) and the
  aromatic rings of Phe/Tyr/His/Trp;
* the **PLANARITY impropers** that keep those aromatic rings flat — needed
  because a freed 6-ring can fold ("boat") and an sp2 substituent can leave
  the plane, neither of which bonds and angles alone can prevent.

Build output on this structure: 1839 stretching, 3004 bending and 6248
dihedral springs, on 13964 ghost particles.

**Omega is deliberately rigid.** `ProteinAtomRigidGroups.rbody`'s
`_PHI`/`_PSI` cliques span the whole peptide plane, which freezes omega at
whatever value the input structure carries — a cis input stays cis, a trans
input stays trans, exactly and indefinitely (spring equilibria are the
distances measured at load time). The `.bi.ff`'s omega rings are built and
are the only construction that could restore the real cis/trans double well
if that freedom is ever wanted; real-atom springs provably cannot (every
substituent across a peptide bond is coplanar with it, so the energy of any
spring set is convex in cos(omega) — one minimum, never two).


## Naming caveat on the Lennard-Jones mode

`steric.mode = lennard-jones-8-6Amber` (stages 2-4) actually implements a
classic **LJ 12-6**, matching the real AMBER force field — the "8-6" in the
name is factually wrong. It is corrected on the branch
`fix/rename-lennard-jones-amber-12-6` (renamed to
`lennard-jones-12-6Amber`); use the 8-6 spelling as long as that branch is
not merged. The other modes (Lewitt, Zacharias) genuinely *are* 8-6.


## A bug this example uncovered, and its fix

Writing stage 2 immediately showed `Steric energy: 0.00` with everything
apparently configured correctly. The cause was a single missing line in
`Topology::to_spring_network` (`src/topology/Topology.hpp`): it copied
mass, charge and radius from the topology particle to the simulation
particle, but **not epsilon**, which therefore stayed at its 0.0 default.
Since every Lennard-Jones form is proportional to `epsilon_ij`, the steric
term produced exactly zero energy *and* exactly zero force — silently, with
no warning, on any topology built this way. Every other link in the chain
was already correct (the `.nbi.ff` reader parses epsilon, `Reducer` sets it
on the grain, `SpnBuffer` writes it, `NetCDFReader` restores it).

The fix is in place. If you are comparing against older results, note that
this means **steric exclusion was inert in earlier runs**, including
`example/071`'s — its refolding behaviour was driven by Coulomb and
hydrogen bonds alone.


## Performance, and what dominates it

Single-threaded step rates measured on this structure:

| stage           | steps/s | comment                                    |
|-----------------|---------|--------------------------------------------|
| bonded only     | ~550    | no neighbour search at all                  |
| full model      | ~6      | dominated by the non-bonded neighbour search |

The spring model is not the bottleneck: per evaluated term, a distance
spring is cheaper than the trigonometric bonded routine it replaces (see
the microbenchmark in `doc/BondedForceFieldSprings.md`). Against OpenMM's
CPU platform on the same structure, single-threaded, BioSpring's bonded
model is ~3x slower (it evaluates more primitives: ghost placements, force
redistributions, per-pair rings), while the non-bonded gap is far larger —
that is BioSpring's grid/cutoff implementation and its 16 A electrostatic
cutoff, unrelated to the bonded work. Build with OpenMP for interactive
use.


## Notes on the physics you will see

* **This is a quench, not a thermostatted ensemble.** `viscosity` damps
  without a compensating random force, so trajectories relax toward the
  nearest local minimum and stay there. Do not read equilibrium populations
  (rotamer statistics, folding free energies) off these runs.
* Consequently, an unfolded structure will not spontaneously fold: crossing
  a real barrier needs thermal activation the model does not provide. Pull
  it interactively instead (MDDriver is wired in the Makefile, same as
  examples 070/071).
* The per-family `.msp` switches (`bending.enable`, `dihedralphi.enable`,
  `dihedralpsi.enable`, `dihedralomega.enable`, `dihedralchi.enable`) gate
  at runtime whichever families were built, which is handy for isolating
  one term's contribution to the reported energy channels without
  rebuilding.


## Files

* `gkinase.pdb` — the structure (all 20 amino acids).
* `amber.hbond` — donor/acceptor table for stage 4, a local copy of
  `data/reducerules/amber.hbond` (keyed by `amber.grp`'s renamed types, so
  it must stay in step with the `--grp` used at build time).
* `model_bonded.msp`, `model_lj.msp`, `model_lj_coulomb.msp`,
  `model_full.msp` — one per stage, each documenting its own additions.
* `model.vmd` — visualization; renames backbone atoms back to N/CA/C/O so
  VMD's STRIDE works, draws every spring from the CONECT records, and
  carries a (hidden by default) representation of the ghost particles.
